      "4 Three modes and overall flow (4.1 proportionality gate, 4.2 three depths, 4.3 boundary-crossing view, 4.4 closed verification contract, 4.5 pre-execution logic scenarios, 4.6 responsibility completeness)",
      "5 Foundation-planning mode (5.1-5.6)",
      "6 Technical inquiry and decision (6.1-6.4)",
      "7 Incremental implementation mode (7.1-7.5)",
      "8 Verification: contract, delivery, behavior, effect (8.1-8.4)",
      "9 Swamp-escape mode (9.1-9.4)",
      "10 Cross-validation as independent refutation",
      "11 Execution rules for AI",
      "12 Self-check of this framework (incl. 12.1)",
      "13 One-sentence summary",
      "14 References"
    ]
  },
  "selectedOption": {
    "id": "domain-renderer-record-only",
    "description": "A record-only target (targets/product) reusing the existing generic record-only core runtime, with a domain-owned answer contract and renderer. The AI owns meaning (what wording is preserved, what is in scope, what stays unknown); the contract owns decidable structure (required unknowns field, single-line fields, closed shape); the renderer owns the exact brief format and managed-region bytes; the generic runtime owns fail-closed input/output declaration, staleness, locking, and observed-effect reporting.",
    "why": "The record-only mechanism already exists in core, so this option adds only domain files (config, config schema, answer contract, renderer, entry) and removes concrete prose costs: the AI no longer re-derives the brief format each use, unknowns cannot be silently dropped (required contract field always rendered), bytes outside the managed region cannot be clobbered, and a missing request or stale input fails closed instead of inviting guesses."
  },
  "rejectedOptions": [
    {
      "id": "prose-only",
      "why": "A prose target cannot declare inputs/output (target schema gives prose mode no declaredInputs/declaredOutput), so nothing fails closed when pilot/product-request.md is missing; the AI hand-writes pilot/brief.md each time, risking clobbered user bytes and silently dropped unknowns; the representative failure (paraphrased wording, guessed decisions) is guarded only by instructions."
    },
    {
      "id": "core-generic-mechanism",
      "why": "Adding a new generic brief/template mechanism to src/core or src/runtime would change shared core for one domain need with no observed repeated cost across domains; the existing record-only core already provides the needed generic mechanism, so new core machinery fails the entry test in SKILL.md step 6 and Framework 9.2."
    },
    {
      "id": "independent-alternative-static-template",
      "why": "A hand-maintained pilot/brief.md template plus checklist outside Skill Rails is genuinely independent but loses deterministic build, receipt, and provenance the user's purpose requires; it creates a second canonical owner of domain meaning next to the request, and hand edits can silently drop unknowns with no fail-closed boundary."
    }
  ],
  "prohibitionsRespected": {
    "prepare": "Absent. record-only mode; generated tree has no prepare.mjs and config/target.json has packet=null.",
